module Proof
  # The written form of a size, such as `"8GB"` or `"1.5 GiB"`, read into
  # whole megabytes.
  #
  # `Proof::Resources.request` reads a memory size with it. It is a separate
  # module so that the form is checked in one place, and so that `Resources`
  # holds only what a request is. It is a private constant of `Proof`, so the
  # domain has no public name for it: read a size with
  # `Proof::Resources.request`.
  #
  # A size is a number, whole or decimal, an optional space, and a unit: `MB`,
  # `GB`, `TB`, `MiB`, `GiB`, or `TiB`, in any case. Every unit counts in
  # 1,024s, so `1GB` is 1,024 MB and `1TB` is 1,048,576 MB. A size that isn't a
  # whole number of megabytes rounds up to the next whole megabyte, so a
  # request is never smaller than what was written.
  module SizeText
    # The form of a size, with the number and the unit as named groups.
    FORM: Regexp

    # The megabytes in one of each unit, by the unit's first letter in
    # lowercase, as a symbol.
    MEGABYTES: Hash[Symbol, Integer]

    # The megabytes in `text`, rounded up to a whole number, or `nil` when
    # `text` isn't a size in the form above. A value that isn't text, and text
    # with a byte that isn't valid text, give `nil`.
    #
    # - `text`: Object, such as `"8GB"`. Any value is accepted, and only a
    #   String in the form gives a number.
    def self.megabytes: (Object text) -> Integer?
  end
end
